# Photographic Processing
#
# Black-and-white, C-41, ECN-2, and E-6 as sequences of bath states with registry-held temperatures and times, plus the density curve that tells a lab whether its chemistry is in control.

#>media_carriers_processing
#:layer #!pragmatics
#:domain #!media_carriers
#:form #!protocol
#:level #!advanced
#:review #!adversarial_checked
#:valence #!bone
#:era #!modern

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Photographic Processing"
 ~#claim: "Each process is a fixed sequence of baths; ECN-2 develops at 41.1 C for 3 minutes, C-41 at 37.8 C, and densities are read by ISO 5-2 geometry to keep it in control."
 ~#proof: "Kodak process manuals H-24.07, Z-131, and Z-119 for the baths; Hurter and Driffield 1890 and ISO 5-2 for density; James for development chemistry."
 ~#door: "Stack tracing paper to see why density adds, then walk one process from first bath to last."
 ~#audience: "lab technicians, cinematographers, film students, and home developers"
 continuity: "Photographic Processing | fixed sequence of baths"
 cold_open: "Every film process is a fixed sequence of baths, and a strip of known exposures tells the lab when the chemistry drifts."
}

